I think the RCFT hypothesis is substantially clearer now than it was even a few simulation generations ago, because several ideas that originally looked like one large claim have separated into distinct mathematical layers.

The most important refinement is this: **RCFT should not presently be presented as “an \(Sp(8)\)-octonion-\(E_8\)-\(H_4\) theory” in which all of those structures are simply identified with one another.** The stronger formulation is that RCFT has an operational stochastic core, an \(Sp(8,\mathbb R)\) parent phase-space hypothesis, a candidate octonion-inspired operator realization, and a proposed mechanism by which locally associative quaternionic sectors could generate an effective \(H_4\)-organized observable layer.

That separation is what makes the program falsifiable.

# RCFT: Current Unified Working Hypothesis

At its most compact, I would now write the architecture schematically as

\[
\boxed{
\text{symplectic relational substrate}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{local admissibility}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{history-dependent stochastic paths}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{coherent low-valence sectors}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{associative quaternionic locality}
\;\longrightarrow\;
H_4\text{-type projected organization}.
}
\]

The algebraic version of the same proposal is approximately

\[
\boxed{
Sp(8,\mathbb R)
+\mathcal R_{E_8}
+\text{octonion-inspired local operators}
\xrightarrow{\;\mathcal L,\;\Pi\;}
\mathbb H\text{-sectors}
\xrightarrow{\text{closure}}
H_4 .
}
\]

Every arrow there is important, because several of those arrows are still conjectures rather than proved equivalences.

---

# 1. The operational core: RCFT does not begin with geometry

The strongest part of RCFT remains the part that does **not** require octonions, \(E_8\), or even \(H_4\).

There is a relational configuration state

\[
R_t,
\]

an admissible set of candidate transitions

\[
R_t\rightarrow R_{t+1},
\]

a history-dependent memory state, and a local evaluation process determining how compatible each candidate transition is with the current relational configuration.

The realized path is stochastic,

\[
R_0\rightarrow R_1\rightarrow R_2\rightarrow\cdots,
\]

but the rules defining transition weights need not themselves be random.

That gives RCFT the important distinction

\[
\boxed{
\text{deterministic admissibility law}
\neq
\text{deterministic realized trajectory}.
}
\]

This is one of the better-developed conceptual pieces of the theory.

RCFT can therefore have a deterministic algebraic/operator substrate while producing probabilistic observational histories.

---

# 2. Indivisible Stochastic Processes and non-Markovian evolution

The ISP language now has a fairly clear purpose.

RCFT is not describing a sequence of independently sampled states. The transition event is the primitive object. A transition cannot in general be factored conceptually into an independently existing starting object, interaction, and destination state without losing information about the relational event.

More importantly, the dynamics are non-Markovian:

\[
P(R_{t+1}\mid R_t)
\neq
P(R_{t+1}\mid R_t,R_{t-1},\ldots)
\]

in general.

A more faithful notation would therefore be

\[
P(R_{t+1}\mid R_t,\mathcal H_t),
\]

where \(\mathcal H_t\) is the bounded or saturated historical memory available to the LCO/update mechanism.

That is central rather than decorative. Memory changes transition weights, transition weights change visited pathways, and visited pathways rewrite memory.

So there is a feedback loop:

\[
\boxed{
R_t
\rightarrow
\mathcal L
\rightarrow
K_t
\rightarrow
R_{t+1}
\rightarrow
\mathcal H_{t+1}
\rightarrow
\mathcal L_{t+1}.
}
\]

This is one reason I would be very careful about trying to replace RCFT too early with an ordinary equilibrium-energy picture.

The current ISP interpretation is that an allowed transition is treated as an indivisible relational event. The rule determining admissibility can be deterministic while the realized trajectory remains stochastic.

Schematically,

\[
K_t(R_i\rightarrow R_j)
=
\frac{
\exp\!\left[-F_{ij}(t)/T_{\mathrm{tol}}\right]
}{
\sum_{k\in N(i)}
\exp\!\left[-F_{ik}(t)/T_{\mathrm{tol}}\right]
}.
\]

The current toy friction architecture has essentially been

\[
F_{ij}
=
\alpha
+
\beta \Delta_{\Pi}(i,j)
-
\lambda M_{ij}^{\mathrm{sat}},
\]

with sign conventions adjustable according to the exact implementation.

Here:

- \(\beta\) penalizes transitions crossing projection/sector structure;
- \(M_{ij}^{\mathrm{sat}}\) contains historical reinforcement;
- and \(T_{\mathrm{tol}}\) sets stochastic transition bandwidth.

This gives a useful conceptual distinction:

\[
\boxed{
\text{deterministic transition law}
\neq
\text{deterministic realized trajectory}.
}
\]

The kernel is determined once the instantaneous relational state and memory are specified, while the sampled transition remains probabilistic.

That is an important part of RCFT and, in my view, one of the cleaner pieces of the architecture.

### A mathematical refinement

There is also a useful subtlety here. Although \(R_t\) alone is non-Markovian, the enlarged state

\[
Z_t=(R_t,M_t)
\]

may be Markovian if the memory variable \(M_t\) contains everything required to determine the next transition.

That is excellent for the proof program.

Rather than trying to develop stochastic-stability theory directly on an awkward non-Markov process, we may be able to prove things on the **augmented state space**:

\[
Z_t\rightarrow Z_{t+1}.
\]

This is precisely where standard stochastic Lyapunov/Foster-Lyapunov machinery could eventually enter.

