# 5. Valence

Valence may be one of the most important additions made during SIM9–10.

Effective valence can be expressed through transition entropy:

\[
V_{\mathrm{eff},x}
=
\exp(H_{\mathrm{transition},x}).
\]

This is especially useful because it gives a continuous notion of how many pathways are effectively available even when the graph itself has fixed combinatorial degree.

High valence means many competing pathways remain dynamically viable.

Low valence means the transition distribution has concentrated onto fewer directions.

RCFT therefore appears to exhibit something like

\[
\boxed{
\text{coherence increase}
\quad\leftrightarrow\quad
\text{effective valence contraction},
}
\]

although this should not yet be elevated to a theorem.

That observation is much more interesting than merely saying “ordered states have lower entropy,” because valence gives the ordering an explicitly relational interpretation:

**coherent states are states in which fewer relational continuations remain competitively admissible.**

That is potentially fundamental to the projection story.

Valence was the missing variable that substantially changed the stability investigation.

The effective valence being measured is

\[
V_{\mathrm{eff},x}
=
e^{H_{\mathrm{loc},x}},
\]

where \(H_{\mathrm{loc},x}\) is the entropy of the local outgoing transition distribution.

Interpretationally:

\[
V_{\mathrm{eff}}\approx 1
\]

means that effectively one direction dominates;

larger \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\) means that several outgoing alternatives remain dynamically available.

The simulations show a clear relationship:

\[
\beta\uparrow
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
V_{\mathrm{eff}}\downarrow
\]

while local coherence generally increases.

That makes valence contraction a serious candidate component of local stability.

It also fits the broader RCFT idea that coherent structures form by progressively restricting the set of dynamically competitive alternatives rather than by globally minimizing an externally imposed energy function.

