# 7. Epsilon

\(\epsilon\) also became more mathematically meaningful during this process.

It should not be interpreted as simply “randomness.”

It is best regarded as a controlled displacement in relational/operator space:

\[
X\rightarrow X+\epsilon\,\delta X.
\]

Depending upon the experiment, \(X\) may mean the state, memory configuration, or effective transition kernel.

So the relevant quantity becomes a response:

\[
\frac{\Delta L}{\epsilon}.
\]

In the true infinitesimal limit we would like to investigate

\[
\lim_{\epsilon\rightarrow0}
\frac{\Delta L}{\epsilon}.
\]

That begins to approximate a directional derivative of the stability functional.

There is one technical issue in the existing simulations that should eventually be cleaned up: not all state perturbations implemented so far genuinely have continuous \(\epsilon\)-magnitude. Some use \(\epsilon\) as the *probability* of performing a finite jump. Those are useful perturbation tests, but they aren't literal differential probes.

A future implementation should distinguish

\[
\epsilon_{\mathrm{probability}}
\]

from

\[
\epsilon_{\mathrm{distance}}.
\]

That will matter once we claim an actual restoring field.

