# 8. What SIM2–SIM10 Have Taught Us

The simulations should be viewed as a progression rather than as isolated experiments.

They have gradually moved RCFT from

\[
\text{“interesting memory-dependent network behavior”}
\]

toward

\[
\text{“a measurable local stability architecture.”}
\]

## SIM2 — Friction and projection begin producing organization

The early simulations established that a transition system with

- projection penalties,
- relational friction,
- stochastic transition weights,

can generate nonuniform occupancy and persistent relational behavior.

The important early lesson was that projection penalties do not merely suppress transitions uniformly.

They restructure the available transition landscape.

This gave the first computational basis for treating

\[
\beta
\]

as a parameter controlling relational admissibility rather than simply a generic energy penalty.

---

## SIM3–SIM4 — Memory becomes dynamically consequential

The memory sector showed that previous traversals alter future transition weights.

This established the genuinely non-Markovian part of the engine.

Memory could reinforce particular pathways and generate history-sensitive asymmetry even on a fixed underlying graph.

The major lesson was

\[
\boxed{
\text{topology alone does not determine RCFT dynamics}.
}
\]

The same graph can support different effective relational structures depending on traversal history.

---

## SIM5 — Ordering and transition entropy

By this stage, conditional transition entropy became a useful diagnostic.

The simulations showed that increased relational ordering could be detected through reductions in quantities like

\[
H_{\mathrm{cond}}.
\]

This gave a quantitative measure for the idea that the system was becoming less dynamically diverse and more locally predictable.

Importantly, this was not merely static state entropy.

It was entropy of the **transition structure**.

---

## SIM6 — Persistence, dwell, and memory concentration

The next results strengthened the connection among

- memory reinforcement,
- dwell time,
- localized loops,
- and concentrated transition weights.

The emerging picture became

\[
\boxed{
\text{history}
\rightarrow
\text{reinforcement}
\rightarrow
\text{persistent local pathways}.
}
\]

This was one of the first signs that attractor-like relational objects might arise without ordinary global potential minimization.

---

## SIM7 — Coherence band across \(\beta\)

The \(\beta\)-sweeps showed a repeatable transition toward

- lower conditional entropy,
- lower effective number of active pathways,
- increased dwell,
- and stronger ordering.

This gave evidence for a **coherence regime** rather than merely one tuned parameter point.

That was important because it suggested the observed structure was not a numerical accident at one isolated value of \(\beta\).

---

## SIM8 — Local coherence and basin differentiation

SIM8 was a major conceptual shift.

Basin-level quantities

\[
C_x=-H_x+\alpha P_x
\]

rose systematically with \(\beta\).

At the same time, local transition kernels became **more different from one another**.

This was surprising and useful.

Local scalar coherence became more similar while local dynamics specialized.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\text{coherence}
\neq
\text{uniformity}.
}
\]

The system appeared capable of becoming globally more organized while its local sectors became dynamically more differentiated.

That led to the idea of

\[
\boxed{
\text{compatible differentiation}.
}
\]

This was also the point where the possibility of a fundamentally **local coherence architecture** became much stronger.

---

## SIM9.1 — The global residual artifact

SIM9.1 attempted to construct a global kernel and compare basin kernels against it.

The result was

\[
R_{\mathrm{global}}=0.
\]

At first glance this looked striking, but analysis showed that the result was guaranteed by construction.

The global kernel had effectively been built as the union/average of the same basin kernels that were then being reconstructed.

Therefore

\[
P_x
=
\Pi_x(P_{\mathrm{global}})
\]

by definition.

This was not evidence for perfect global consistency.

It was an important **artifact diagnosis**.

That episode improved the methodology because it showed that any proposed “global structure” must be independently defined rather than reconstructed tautologically from the local pieces being tested against it.

---

## SIM9.2 — Observable-to-hidden reconstruction and specialization

SIM9.2 used a less trivial construction.

The global object was built at the observable basin level and then lifted back toward hidden-state dynamics.

This generated a nonzero reconstruction residual.

As \(\beta\) increased,

\[
C_{\mathrm{local}}\uparrow
\]

while the reconstruction residual also increased.

That suggested that locally coherent sectors were becoming **more specialized**, not more mean-field-like.

The result therefore weakened the naive hypothesis

\[
\text{coherence}
\Rightarrow
\text{convergence toward one global kernel}.
\]

Instead it favored

\[
\boxed{
\text{local coherence}
+
\text{dynamical specialization}.
}
\]

---

## SIM9.3 — Burn-in, perturbation, and recovery

SIM9.3 tested whether the local coherence scalar itself behaved like a restoring Lyapunov quantity.

The initial burn-in drift was negative because the simulation began in an artificially perfectly predictable state and then explored the graph.

After perturbation, however,

\[
\Delta C_{\mathrm{local}}
\]

was generally extremely close to zero rather than robustly restoring.

This was an important negative result.

It told us

\[
\boxed{
C_x\text{ alone is probably not the Lyapunov variable}.
}
\]

That forced the search toward additional degrees of freedom.

---

## SIM9.4 — Valence enters

SIM9.4 measured effective valence.

A very clean trend appeared:

\[
\beta\uparrow
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
V_{\mathrm{eff}}\downarrow,
\]

while

\[
C_x\uparrow.
\]

This was one of the clearest empirical relationships in the sequence.

It suggested that relational stability might involve a coupled quantity

\[
(C_x,V_{\mathrm{eff},x})
\]

rather than coherence by itself.

That result changed the conceptual interpretation of the LCO.

The LCO may not merely reward “high coherence.”

It may determine which degree of **local branching** remains admissible.

---

## SIM9.5 — \(\epsilon\)-response

SIM9.5 introduced controlled perturbations.

State, kernel, and memory perturbations were applied with different \(\epsilon\).

The response of \(C_x\) alone remained inconsistent.

Some basins recovered positively, some negatively, and many responses were nearly neutral.

This further weakened the single-variable Lyapunov interpretation.

At the same time, the perturbation experiment established the right methodology:

\[
\boxed{
\text{burn in}
\rightarrow
\text{perturb}
\rightarrow
\text{measure recovery}.
}
\]

That methodology is likely to remain central.

---

## SIM9.6 — Coupled coherence–valence functional

SIM9.6 tested

\[
L_{\gamma,x}
=
C_x
-
\gamma\log V_{\mathrm{eff},x}
-
\lambda_RR_{\mathrm{global}}.
\]

The important discovery was that the sign of the recovery drift changed systematically with

\[
\beta
\]

and

\[
\gamma.
\]

That demonstrated that

\[
C_x
\]

and

\[
V_{\mathrm{eff},x}
\]

cannot simply be treated as independent diagnostics.

Their combination determines the orientation of the restoring response.

This produced the concept of a

\[
\boxed{
\text{Lyapunov stability zone}.
}
\]

Crucially, \(\gamma\) should not be interpreted as a freely tunable physical knob.

It is an experimental coordinate used to discover what sort of coherence–valence coupling the true LCO would have to realize.

---

## SIM10 — Basin-resolved stability geometry

SIM10 moved from one basin and a few parameter points to a broader map over

\[
(\beta,\gamma,\epsilon,\text{perturbation class}).
\]

It measured the basin vector

\[
(\Delta L_0,\Delta L_1,\Delta L_2,\Delta L_3)
\]

and summarized it using

\[
\overline{\Delta L},
\qquad
\sigma_L,
\]

and basin extremes.

The most important lesson from SIM10 is not a single universal “stable \(\gamma\).”

Instead, the sign structure changes with \(\beta\).

Different dynamical regimes exhibit different coherence–valence orientations.

That means the admissible Lyapunov direction is likely **state/regime dependent**.

The data therefore support a picture of

\[
\boxed{
\text{a basin-local stability field rather than one universal global scalar}.
}
\]

SIM10 also showed that perturbation class matters:

- state perturbations,
- kernel perturbations,
- memory perturbations

need not produce identical restoring behavior.

This is exactly what one would expect in a genuinely non-Markovian theory because memory-space displacement is not equivalent to ordinary configuration-space displacement.

