# 10. The emerging stability geometry

The picture now looks more like

\[
\mathbf X_x
=
\begin{pmatrix}
C_x\\
\log V_{\mathrm{eff},x}\\
R_x\\
\cdots
\end{pmatrix}
\]

than like a single one-dimensional energy landscape.

The dynamics define an expected local drift

\[
\mathbf v_x(\mathbf X)
=
\mathbb E[\Delta\mathbf X_x\mid\mathbf X].
\]

A scalar Lyapunov quantity, if one exists, would then be a function

\[
L_x=L_x(\mathbf X_x)
\]

whose gradient has a definite relationship with that drift:

\[
\nabla L_x\cdot\mathbf v_x<0
\]

under the decreasing-Lyapunov convention.

That is, in my view, the mathematically serious version of what SIM8–10 have been hinting at.

The real object may therefore be the **local vector field first**, with the scalar Lyapunov function derived second.

That is worth pursuing before forcing another scalar ansatz.

