# 11. \(Sp(8,\mathbb R)\): the parent dynamical structure

The current RCFT hypothesis takes

\[
Sp(8,\mathbb R)
\]

as the adopted parent symplectic framework.

Here precision matters.

\(Sp(8,\mathbb R)\) acts on an **eight-dimensional real symplectic vector space**

\[
(V,\Omega),
\qquad
\dim_{\mathbb R}V=8,
\]

and preserves

\[
M^T\Omega M=\Omega.
\]

It should not be described as “the symmetry group of octonions.” It isn't.

Nor does \(Sp(8,\mathbb R)\) automatically eliminate octonionic non-associativity.

Its role in RCFT is different:

\[
\boxed{
Sp(8,\mathbb R)
=
\text{associative linear/symplectic carrier of the relational dynamics}.
}
\]

That makes it possible to formulate evolution in ordinary operator composition while using octonion-inspired structure to constrain which operators or sectors are available.

This distinction is crucial.

The fact that

\[
\mathbb O\cong\mathbb R^8
\]

as real vector spaces gives an appealing dimensional compatibility, but it does **not** prove that the octonion algebra naturally carries the required symplectic form.

That compatibility still has to be constructed.

---

# 12. Octonions: what RCFT is really proposing

The best current formulation is not

\[
Sp(8)\text{ acts on octonions and removes nonassociativity}.
\]

It is closer to

\[
\boxed{
\text{octonionic multiplication supplies hidden algebraic relational data,}
}
\]

while

\[
\boxed{
\text{an associative linear-operator realization carries the actual composition dynamics.}
}
\]

For an octonion \(a\), left multiplication

\[
L_a:x\mapsto ax
\]

is an ordinary real-linear map

\[
L_a\in\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(\mathbb O)
\cong M_8(\mathbb R).
\]

Likewise,

\[
R_a:x\mapsto xa.
\]

The matrices themselves compose associatively:

\[
(L_aL_b)L_c
=
L_a(L_bL_c).
\]

But crucially,

\[
L_aL_b
\neq
L_{ab}
\]

in general.

That difference is where octonionic nonassociativity survives inside an associative operator algebra.

This is, mathematically, a much stronger foundation for RCFT than talking about multiplying arbitrary \(8\times8\) octonionic matrices.

In fact, I would actively remove the phrase **“octonionic \(8\times8\) matrix”** from the canonical hypothesis unless a very specific nonassociative matrix algebra is later defined.

What RCFT needs first is an **\(8\times8\) real operator representation of octonionic multiplication**.

That distinction will save you substantial mathematical grief.

---

# 13. Fano-plane local algebra

This is where the 3-fold hypothesis becomes particularly interesting.

Every appropriate Fano-plane line gives an imaginary-unit triad

\[
(e_i,e_j,e_k),
\]

and together with the real unit,

\[
Q_{ijk}
=
\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}
\{1,e_i,e_j,e_k\}
\cong\mathbb H.
\]

Inside this sector,

\[
[a,b,c]=0.
\]

Thus the octonions naturally contain associative four-dimensional quaternionic subalgebras.

That gives RCFT a concrete possible hierarchy:

\[
\boxed{
\mathbb O
\supset
Q_{ijk}\cong\mathbb H.
}
\]

Rather than “throwing away half the octonion,” the LCO/projection mechanism could restrict admissible dynamics to one or a compatible family of quaternionic slices.

That is much more defensible.

